ront end that will handle the  traffic to your website  google cloud https load balancing is a  global proxy based layer 7 load balancer  that enables you to run and scale your  services worldwide behind a single  external ip address  it does much better than dns based load  balancing because there are no ttls  involved and the load balancer can  respond much quicker to changes in  traffic patterns or health of the  backends  it also allows you to do content-based  load balancing using url maps to select  the appropriate backend service  finally you can run within google's  premium tier networking that will offer  cross-regional load balancing and  allowing you to direct traffic to the  closest healthy backend that has  capacity  now it's time to add some security by  adding cloud armor to the incoming  traffic  cloud armor sits at the google edge and  provides ddos protection along with  predefined rules that defend against  crash site scripting and sql injection  attacks  you can also create custom ip based  allow and deny lists and prevent  malicious users from consuming your  resources  here's an overall picture that shows how  everything is put together  with that i hand it over to armand who  will go over the same steps but in much  greater detail  thanks cartech  my name is arman rai and i am a network  specialist at google cloud  for this portion of the presentation i  will walk you through an example  deployment on google cloud from start to  finish  we will go from ordering a domain all  the way through verifying that all the  appropriate features are enabled  let's get started we will begin our demo  with the domain registration process  the first step is to check that the  domain you would like to purchase is  available  running the search domains command will  give you a list of tlds and the  associated prices  even though we have specified a com in  the search the result will give many  tlds that are available for purchase  once you have decided on the domain name  you would like to purchase and confirmed  it is available you need to create a  cloud dns zone with a domain name  this zone is where you will manage dns  for this domain  make sure you set the dns sec state to  on to utilize the security benefits of  dns sec  next you will actually need to order the  domain  the gcloud response from the registrar  command will prompt you to add  information about your business and list  a point of contact  the final step is to link the dns zone  that we created in step 1 to the domain  cloud domains  this will ensure that the appropriate  name server records as well as the  appropriate ds records are added for dns  sec  a great feature of the gcp platform is  that this step is automated for you so  you don't need to do it yourself  we strongly encourage customers to use  gcp for both their dns and registrar  needs due to these increasing product  synergies in this demo we will be  configuring two backends one for static  content like images logos or videos  and the other for dynamic content like  html and any apis your website relies on  let's start with the static content  first we create a storage bucket for  this bucket we have chosen to have it be  regional in our u.s east 1 location and  to use the standard storage class  second we must add some content to the  storage bucket  in a typical deployment there are many  options on how to upload content to a  storage bucket please see our  documentation for further information  about how to do so  third we will update the bucket  permissions to public so that anyone can  access the contents stored  you can additionally leverage im  permissions if you would like to  restrict access to the bucket but we  will not be covering that in this demo  now that we have set up our static  content storage bucket we will pivot to  dynamic traffic  before we can create a managed instance  group we must create a template which  will be the blueprint for all of the  compute we will utilize  there's a lot of metadata you can add in  this template in this example we have  picked a region for the compute to be  deployed  a vpc network that my networking is  utilizing as well as a specific subnet  which i want my compute instances to  utilize we have additionally specialized  specified an image for the compute and a  simple startup script to install apache  and run a basic web server  now that we've created the template for  our dynamic traffic to use we need to  instantiate the actual instance group  the first command creates the instance  group using the template we just created  the second command specifies that we are  running our web application on port 80.  in this demo we are using http for the  back end of our load balancer since all  traffic is on google's private network  if you'd like you can additionally run  https traffic on port 443 for your back  ends  before we get into creating the load  balancer i want to spend a few moments  discussing what are the different  components from a g cloud perspective  that are required starting from the left  in this diagram we need a globally  accessible static ip address this is the  ip address that will correspond to our  load balancer and is linked using a  forwarding rule  second we have the actual proxy itself  we need to create two proxies one for  http and one for https  the global http load balancer is a proxy  load balancer so this step is required  third we need to input some logic that  defines how traffic is routed between  our dynamic backends and our static back  ends  this configuration is called the url map  finally we need to identify the  different ends that we want the traffic  to be sent to  one for our static bucket and one for  our instance group  in addition we need to configure whether  we'd like to use cloud cdn for content  caching or cloud armor for security  there are two steps we need to take  before we start configuring the load  balancer  we need to reserve a static ip address  for our load balancer to use  a common mistake in this step is not to  specify the global flag  once we have reserved it we need to list  it  please save this ip address for later in  the demonstration  it's worth mentioning that as long as  this address this ip address is in your  static ip addresses  this ip will be able to be utilized by  any of your by your resources in your  gcp project  additionally we need to make sure that  the firewall in our vpc is set to allow  traffic from the slash 16 and slash 22  cider ranges listed  if you do not do this step your health  checks will be blocked and no traffic  will be able to reach your back end  now we can get started with configuring  our load balancer  in this first command we create a basic  health check which will probe our back  end on port 80. this command utilizes  the default settings  know that there are a lot of  customizations available beyond the  defaults for health checks but we will  not be covering in them in this session  health checks are a very powerful tool  which allow for only healthy back ends  to receive end user traffic  the second command creates a back-end  service which we will attach to our back  ends too  you need to attach a health check to the  back-end service  once we have created our back-end  service we need to add the back-ends to  it the first command adds our dynamic  back-end service to the back end  the second command creates a back-end  bucket for our static content  additionally we are including the enable  cdn flag in our request  by turning on the cdn we are ensuring  that static content is served globally  from google's edge caches instead of  from the direct from the bucket every  time  by doing so we are greatly increasing  the speed of the website  cloud cdn by default will cache static  file types  google determines file type by looking  at the mime type of the response from  origin  we then map the mime type to a list of  static content mappings that we maintain  if you do not want to utilize this  method for cache control no problem you  can set your own cache control headers  at origin  or you can configure a force cache all  setting  be careful when setting the force cache  all setting because you may  inadvertently end up caching content  that is intended to be dynamic i.e  non-cacheable  the third command here adds a custom  response header to requests for this  back end  the custom response header indicates  whether or not the request was served  from the cdn cache  there are a lot of other custom headers  that can be added in addition to the  cache status  you can additionally add your own  headers and set them to be whatever  you'd like  once the back-ends are created we need  to create a url map  the url map is a construct that is used  to determine traffic routing between our  backends  the first command creates the url map  object  in this command we are specifying that  the default location for traffic to be  sent to  is our dynamic backend  the second command adds the logic that  any traffic destined to the path or  directory slash images  star should instead be sent to the  backend bucket you can add many  different paths here but for our demo  we're just going to use one  additionally it's worth mentioning that  there are many more advanced routing  capabilities available to you beyond  path matching in the global https load  balancer  the full list of attributes that you can  route on in the url map is listed in our  documentation as well as guidance on how  to deploy this  in this section we will create the  proxies and order an ssl certificate to  be served at our edge  the ssl certificate will be issued by  google's certificate authority or let's  encrypt  there are a lot of customizations  available within ssl at gcp you can  specify for example which tls versions  you would like to support  or what cipher suites you would like  clients to support as well  in this example we're going to stick  with the defaults  the first command creates the http proxy  this is linked to the url map which we  created in the previous step  the second command orders an ssl  certificate  there are no validation records we need  to add since we are using cloud dns  and later we will point traffic to the  static ip assigned to this load balancer  the ssl certificate will take a few  minutes to provision  but it will be done automatically  we need to make sure however that we  specify all of the host names that we  want listed here in the certificate  even while the ssl certificate is  provisioning we can use this ssl  certificate  to attach to our https proxy which is  what we are doing in step 3.  the last step of the load balancer is to  attach the static ip to the http and  https proxies step 1 attaches the ip to  the http proxy and step 2 attaches the  ip to the https proxy  by doing this the ip address which is  announced globally will be accessible  from the internet on both ports 80 and  443  we aren't covering it in this demo but  you can do an http to https redirect in  the load balancer you can also set hsts  response headers if you would like all  future requests by the same client to  use https no matter what  one of the last steps in our demo is to  create a google cloud armor policy  cloud armor is google's ddos and web  application firewall solution  before we configure it i want to quickly  explain how cloud armor works and how it  is provisioned  cloud armor similar to cloud cdn is  available as part of the global https  load balancer  cloud armor consists of security  policies  a security policy is a collection of  rules  rules can be custom firewall rules or  particular protections included in the  owasp top 10.  a cloud armor rule has a priority  associated with it  similar to the vpc firewall the lower  the priority number the higher up in the  match hierarchy a given rule is  a security policy is attached to a  back-end service  a back-end service can only have one  security policy associated with it  a security policy can be reused however  across multiple back-end services  to configure cloud armor we must first  create a security policy  the security policy will contain the  rules that are applied to our dynamic  content backend  once the security policy has been  created we can then create an example  rule  the example rule i created will block  any traffic that contains the http  request header called evil header  i have also specified that i would like  to respond to this traffic with a 403  response code  this custom rule utilizes the common  expression language or cell  this is an open source language designed  for simplicity speed safety and  portability  i want to take this moment to  additionally highlight that there is an  implicit rule in cloud armor security  policies which dictates whether traffic  is allowed or blocked by default for  that security policy  if we do not specify this flag in the  first command it will default to an  allow setting  this translates to any traffic that does  not match any of the rules we configure  will be let through  now that we have a security policy and  an example rule  the remaining step is to apply it to a  back end the second step here is an  optional one but a recommended step  it enables cloud armor's adaptive  protection feature  adaptive protection builds machine  learning models which detect and alert  on anomalous application level activity  it additionally generates a signature  describing the attack as well as  generates a custom google waff rule to  block the detected signature  adapt adaptive protection is in public  preview as of the time of this recording  it's worth mentioning as well that the  custom rule that  that adaptive protection generates is  not automatically deployed applied there  is an alerting mechanism and you as the  user can decide whether or not you want  to deploy this rule for your back end  now that everything is set up the only  task 