 the defaults  the first command creates the http proxy  this is linked to the url map which we  created in the previous step  the second command orders an ssl  certificate  there are no validation records we need  to add since we are using cloud dns  and later we will point traffic to the  static ip assigned to this load balancer  the ssl certificate will take a few  minutes to provision  but it will be done automatically  we need to make sure however that we  specify all of the host names that we  want listed here in the certificate  even while the ssl certificate is  provisioning we can use this ssl  certificate  to attach to our https proxy which is  what we are doing in step 3.  the last step of the load balancer is to  attach the static ip to the http and  https proxies step 1 attaches the ip to  the http proxy and step 2 attaches the  ip to the https proxy  by doing this the ip address which is  announced globally will be accessible  from the internet on both ports 80 and  443  we aren't covering it in this demo but  you can do an http to https redirect in  the load balancer you can also set hsts  response headers if you would like all  future requests by the same client to  use https no matter what  one of the last steps in our demo is to  create a google cloud armor policy  cloud armor is google's ddos and web  application firewall solution  before we configure it i want to quickly  explain how cloud armor works and how it  is provisioned  cloud armor similar to cloud cdn is  available as part of the global https  load balancer  cloud armor consists of security  policies  a security policy is a collection of  rules  rules can be custom firewall rules or  particular protections included in the  owasp top 10.  a cloud armor rule has a priority  associated with it  similar to the vpc firewall the lower  the priority number the higher up in the  match hierarchy a given rule is  a security policy is attached to a  back-end service  a back-end service can only have one  security policy associated with it  a security policy can be reused however  across multiple back-end services  to configure cloud armor we must first  create a security policy  the security policy will contain the  rules that are applied to our dynamic  content backend  once the security policy has been  created we can then create an example  rule  the example rule i created will block  any traffic that contains the http  request header called evil header  i have also specified that i would like  to respond to this traffic with a 403  response code  this custom rule utilizes the common  expression language or cell  this is an open source language designed  for simplicity speed safety and  portability  i want to take this moment to  additionally highlight that there is an  implicit rule in cloud armor security  policies which dictates whether traffic  is allowed or blocked by default for  that security policy  if we do not specify this flag in the  first command it will default to an  allow setting  this translates to any traffic that does  not match any of the rules we configure  will be let through  now that we have a security policy and  an example rule  the remaining step is to apply it to a  back end the second step here is an  optional one but a recommended step  it enables cloud armor's adaptive  protection feature  adaptive protection builds machine  learning models which detect and alert  on anomalous application level activity  it additionally generates a signature  describing the attack as well as  generates a custom google waff rule to  block the detected signature  adapt adaptive protection is in public  preview as of the time of this recording  it's worth mentioning as well that the  custom rule that  that adaptive protection generates is  not automatically deployed applied there  is an alerting mechanism and you as the  user can decide whether or not you want  to deploy this rule for your back end  now that everything is set up the only  task we have left to do is to point dns  to the ip we reserved for our load  balancer earlier in the presentation we  highlighted how to reserve and obtain  this static ip address  if you didn't get a chance to write it  down you can always go back and re-list  your static ip addresses for your  project  the dns g cloud integration utilizes a  concept called a transaction  to add any new records you must begin a  transaction  you can see this command in step one  where we start a transaction for our  zone  once the transaction has begun you need  to specify the records you would like to  add  in this example since we are only adding  two records i've explicitly listed them  individually  if you have many records you can upload  your records using a bind file  for this demonstration we will be adding  a www record and an apex record pointing  to the static ip address we configured  earlier  now that we have added the records we  can complete our transaction  once the transaction completes the  change then propagates to our edge and  traffic will be routable for the dub dub  dub and apex domains  we have completed the setup for our demo  now for a final few checks we can make  sure that everything is functioning as  it is supposed to  the first check is perhaps the most  obvious i'm able to access the image we  uploaded to our storage bucket  at the url listed www.gcpwebdemo.com  images snowboarding.jpg  this site is accessible over https i've  included a screenshot of the certificate  information from my browser  no warnings are prompted and the correct  common name is listed  the certificate is valid and has not  expired  additionally you could see that the  organization is google trust services  for this particular certificate  next we will verify that cloud cdn is  running and that static content is being  cached at google's edge as we expected  it to  i've sent a curl to an image in the  storage bucket we created the  snowboarding.jpg  in the response i can see the custom  response header we added gcp-status  with the value hit  this means that the response was served  from a gcp edge cache and not from the  bucket directly  by using cloud cdn you're able to  improve origin offload and reduce egress  costs  while simultaneously increasing the  response speed for the end user  to test that cloud armor is working we  can send a curl request including the  header name evil header  we configured the cloud armor security  policy with a rule that explicitly  blocks traffic with this header  the response from the curl is a 403  forbidden which is exactly what we  expected and consistent with what was  configured  thank you for listening to this  presentation and demo we hope you found  it useful  please add your questions to the  comments if you have any  thanks
 

